Rankine, William John Macquorn
A party of astronomers went measuring of the earth,
And forty million meters they took to be its girth;

Huxley, Julian
By death the moon was gathered in
Long ago, ah long ago;

The intervals between the cosmos are unequal: in some places there
are more, in others fewer; some are growing, others again are dying;
somewhere worlds are coming to be, elsewhere fading.

Gay-Lussac, Joseph Louis
In the study of physics, we see what are called individual facts but which
are by no means isolated and which are not independent of each other;

A t s u c h a
moment I gaze upon it with a mixture of anguish and euphoria; separate
from the universe, as though placed at a certain distance outside it;

Or what if beyond such a determinate space he has left an inﬁnite Vacuum;
to show, how inconsiderable is all that he has made is, to what his Power
could, had he so pleas’d, have produc’d?
